{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'control_plane.notices'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{#
	**What this installation is telling its customers** (XIV-120, §8.16).

	The screen the ticket exists for: whoever runs an installation could see every
	customer and had no way to say anything to them. Everything an operator knew
	that a customer needed — a maintenance window, a module that gained a field, a
	trial about to end — was an email somebody sent by hand if they remembered.

	**A notice is written here and read in the customer's own installation**, and
	nothing copies it between the two. That is the opposite of the purchase screen
	next door, where the row is written by a customer and collected: §4.4 gives a
	customer-facing instance `SELECT` on the registry and no write privilege
	anywhere, so reading is free and writing is impossible. Every row on this page
	is therefore current rather than "as of the last collection", which is why —
	unlike the purchase screen — there is no time printed beside each one.

	**The page is built around what the operator has to be able to believe.** A
	notice nobody sees is worse than none, because the sender believes they have
	told somebody; so the two facts stated everywhere here are *what is live* and
	*who it went to*. What is deliberately absent is whether anybody has read one —
	that needs a fact out of every customer's database, which is XIV-102's
	collector pointed the other way, and §8.16 names it as a gap rather than
	implying it is coming.

	**And it is not a marketing tool.** No image upload, no rich text, no preview
	styled like a campaign. A title, a body, who it is for and when it stops.
#}
{% block body %}
	<main class="container page-shell py-4">
		{{ include('@XiviControlPlane/_nav.html.twig', {current: 'control_plane_notices'}) }}

		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		{#
			The line to read before anything else, and only when there is
			something to read: this installation is currently saying something to
			its customers. Absent at zero, because a banner that always says "0
			live" is furniture and furniture is what the eye learns to skip —
			the tenant list's argument, two pages over.
		#}
		{% if live is not empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-info d-flex align-items-start gap-2" role="alert">
				<i class="bi bi-megaphone" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<div>
					<strong>{{ 'control_plane.notices_live'|trans({'%count%': live|length}) }}</strong>
					<div class="small">{{ 'control_plane.notices_live_hint'|trans }}</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		<div class="card mb-4">
			<div class="card-body">
				<h2 class="h6">{{ 'control_plane.notice_write'|trans }}</h2>

				<form method="post" action="{{ path('control_plane_notice_publish') }}">
					<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('notice') }}">

					<div class="mb-3">
						<label class="form-label" for="notice-title">{{ 'control_plane.notice_title'|trans }}</label>
						<input class="form-control" id="notice-title" name="title" maxlength="200" required>
					</div>

					<div class="mb-3">
						<label class="form-label" for="notice-body">{{ 'control_plane.notice_body'|trans }}</label>
						{#
							Plain text, and the field says so. No editor, no
							markdown, no link: the moment a notice can carry a link
							it is a channel somebody can be phished through, on the
							one screen in the product a customer has no reason to
							distrust.
						#}
						<textarea class="form-control" id="notice-body" name="body" rows="4" required></textarea>
						<div class="form-text">{{ 'control_plane.notice_body_hint'|trans }}</div>
					</div>

					<div class="row g-3">
						<div class="col-md-6">
							<fieldset>
								{#
									Who inside a customer's installation reads it,
									per notice rather than per installation: a
									maintenance window is everybody and a trial
									ending is whoever pays. §8.16 has why the second
									case is `ROLE_ADMIN` and why that coarseness is
									stated rather than dressed up.
								#}
								<legend class="form-label">{{ 'control_plane.notice_audience'|trans }}</legend>
								<div class="form-check">
									<input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="audience" id="audience-everyone"
									       value="everyone" checked>
									<label class="form-check-label" for="audience-everyone">
										{{ 'notice.audience.everyone'|trans }}
									</label>
								</div>
								<div class="form-check">
									<input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="audience" id="audience-admins"
									       value="administrators">
									<label class="form-check-label" for="audience-admins">
										{{ 'notice.audience.administrators'|trans }}
									</label>
								</div>
							</fieldset>
						</div>

						<div class="col-md-6">
							<label class="form-label" for="notice-expires">
								{{ 'control_plane.notice_expires'|trans }}
							</label>
							<input class="form-control" type="datetime-local" id="notice-expires" name="expires_at">
							{#
								Read as UTC, said out loud, because a
								`datetime-local` carries no zone and guessing the
								operator's would make the stored moment depend on
								where somebody was sitting. Customers see it in
								their own zone regardless (§8.4.4).
							#}
							<div class="form-text">{{ 'control_plane.notice_expires_hint'|trans }}</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="row g-3 mt-0">
						<div class="col-md-6">
							<fieldset>
								{#
									**Where it lands** (XIV-166), and the two options
									are two channels rather than one with a dial.
									A dashboard notice is read when a customer opens
									their landing page; an every-page notice is a band
									they cannot walk away from and cannot put away.
									The hint says the second half out loud, because an
									operator picking the loud one should know they are
									taking something rather than adding something.
								#}
								<legend class="form-label">{{ 'control_plane.notice_reach'|trans }}</legend>
								<div class="form-check">
									<input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="reach" id="reach-dashboard"
									       value="dashboard" checked>
									<label class="form-check-label" for="reach-dashboard">
										{{ 'notice.reach.dashboard'|trans }}
									</label>
								</div>
								<div class="form-check">
									<input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="reach" id="reach-every-page"
									       value="every_page">
									<label class="form-check-label" for="reach-every-page">
										{{ 'notice.reach.every_page'|trans }}
									</label>
								</div>
								<div class="form-text">{{ 'control_plane.notice_reach_hint'|trans }}</div>
							</fieldset>
						</div>

						<div class="col-md-6">
							{#
								**How loud it is drawn, on the every-page channel
								only.** A select rather than four radios: four is
								where a radio group stops being a glance and starts
								being a list, and the default is right almost every
								time.

								The hint says where this shows up, because a colour
								an operator chose and then cannot find is worse than
								no colour at all. §8.16 kept the dashboard's cards at
								one weight on purpose, and this is the one place an
								operator would otherwise discover that by accident.
							#}
							<label class="form-label" for="notice-priority">
								{{ 'control_plane.notice_priority'|trans }}
							</label>
							<select class="form-select" id="notice-priority" name="priority">
								<option value="info" selected>{{ 'notice.priority.info'|trans }}</option>
								<option value="warning">{{ 'notice.priority.warning'|trans }}</option>
								<option value="success">{{ 'notice.priority.success'|trans }}</option>
								<option value="danger">{{ 'notice.priority.danger'|trans }}</option>
							</select>
							<div class="form-text">{{ 'control_plane.notice_priority_hint'|trans }}</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<fieldset class="mt-3">
						{#
							Everybody, or named customers. Two states rather than
							"leave the boxes empty for everybody": an addressed
							notice whose recipients had all been deprovisioned
							would otherwise become an announcement to the entire
							installation, silently.
						#}
						<legend class="form-label">{{ 'control_plane.notice_addressed'|trans }}</legend>
						<div class="form-check">
							<input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="every_tenant" id="scope-all"
							       value="1" checked>
							<label class="form-check-label" for="scope-all">
								{{ 'control_plane.notice_every_tenant'|trans }}
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="form-check">
							<input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="every_tenant" id="scope-named"
							       value="0">
							<label class="form-check-label" for="scope-named">
								{{ 'control_plane.notice_named_tenants'|trans }}
							</label>
						</div>

						{% if tenants is empty %}
							<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0 mt-2">
								{{ 'control_plane.notice_no_tenants'|trans }}
							</p>
						{% else %}
							<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-3 g-1 mt-1">
								{% for tenant in tenants %}
									<div class="col">
										<div class="form-check">
											<input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" name="tenants[]"
											       id="tenant-{{ tenant.slug }}" value="{{ tenant.slug }}">
											<label class="form-check-label" for="tenant-{{ tenant.slug }}">
												{{ tenant.name }}
												<span class="small text-body-secondary font-monospace">{{ tenant.slug }}</span>
											</label>
										</div>
									</div>
								{% endfor %}
							</div>
						{% endif %}
					</fieldset>

					<button class="btn btn-primary mt-3" type="submit">
						{{ 'control_plane.notice_publish'|trans }}
					</button>
				</form>
			</div>
		</div>

		{% if notices is empty %}
			<div class="card">
				<div class="card-body">
					<h2 class="h6">{{ 'control_plane.no_notices'|trans }}</h2>
					<p class="text-body-secondary mb-0">{{ 'control_plane.no_notices_hint'|trans }}</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% else %}
			<div class="table-responsive">
				<table class="table align-middle">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.notice_title'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.notice_addressed'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.notice_audience'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.notice_published_at'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.notice_state'|trans }}</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{% for notice in notices %}
							{#
								An expired notice is dimmed rather than removed.
								"What did we tell them in March" is a question
								somebody asks, and a list that answered it by
								having no row would answer it wrongly.
							#}
							<tr class="{{ notice.live ? '' : 'opacity-75' }}">
								<td>
									<div>{{ notice.title }}</div>
									<div class="small text-body-secondary">{{ notice.body|slice(0, 120) }}{{ notice.body|length > 120 ? '…' }}</div>
								</td>
								<td>
									{#
										**Who it went to**, which is half of what
										this screen is for. Named customers are
										listed rather than counted — a number would
										make an operator open something else to find
										out whether the right four companies were on
										it.
									#}
									{% if notice.everyTenant %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">{{ 'control_plane.notice_every_tenant'|trans }}</span>
									{% else %}
										<div class="small">
											{{ 'control_plane.notice_addressed_count'|trans({'%count%': notice.recipients|length}) }}
										</div>
										<div class="small text-body-secondary font-monospace">
											{{- notice.recipients|map(recipient => recipient.slug)|join(', ') -}}
										</div>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								{#
									Who, where, and how loud, stacked in one cell
									rather than spread over three columns (XIV-166).
									They are one answer to one question, *what kind of
									interruption was this*, and a table that made an
									operator read across three columns to assemble it
									would be a table nobody assembles.

									The reach is emphasised when it is the loud one and
									left plain when it is not, which is the opposite of
									badging both: the dashboard is what almost every
									row says, and marking the ordinary case teaches the
									eye to skip the mark.
								#}
								<td class="small">
									<div>{{ notice.audienceKey|trans }}</div>
									<div class="{{ notice.everyPage ? 'fw-semibold' : 'text-body-secondary' }}">
										{{- notice.reachKey|trans -}}
									</div>
									{#
										The priority is printed only for the channel
										that draws it. On a dashboard notice it is a
										stored value nothing renders, and putting it
										here would tell an operator their choice had an
										effect somewhere they have not looked.
									#}
									{% if notice.everyPage %}
										<div class="text-body-secondary">{{ notice.priorityKey|trans }}</div>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td>
									<div>{{ notice.publishedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</div>
									<div class="small text-body-secondary">{{ notice.authorLabel }}</div>
								</td>
								<td>
									{# Colour and the word, never colour alone — the tenant list's rule. #}
									{% if notice.live %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-success">{{ 'control_plane.notice_live'|trans }}</span>
									{% else %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">{{ 'control_plane.notice_ended'|trans }}</span>
									{% endif %}

									{% if notice.expiresAt %}
										<div class="small text-body-secondary">
											{{- 'control_plane.notice_until'|trans({'%time%': notice.expiresAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) -}}
										</div>
									{% endif %}

									{% if notice.live %}
										<form method="post" class="mt-1"
										      action="{{ path('control_plane_notice_withdraw', {id: notice.id}) }}">
											<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('notice') }}">
											<button class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" type="submit">
												{{ 'control_plane.notice_withdraw'|trans }}
											</button>
										</form>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
							</tr>
						{% endfor %}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			{#
				What this page cannot tell you, written on it rather than left to
				be assumed. An operator reading a list of live notices will want to
				know who has seen them, and the honest answer is that nothing here
				knows — see §8.16.
			#}
			<p class="text-body-secondary small">{{ 'control_plane.notices_no_receipts'|trans }}</p>
		{% endif %}
	</main>
{% endblock %}
